        "explanation": "● Asymmetric federalism is based on unequal powers and relationships in political, administrative, and \nfiscal arrangement spheres between the federal units constituting a federation. Asymmetry in the \narrangements of a federation can be viewed in both vertical (between the Centre and States) and \nhorizontal (among the States) senses. \n● Some Union Territories have elected legislatures while others are governed directly by the Union. \nUnlike Jammu and Kashmir, Delhi and Puducherry that have their own legislative assemblies, other UTs \nare administered entirely by the Centre. The variation in political institutions across UTs reflects \nasymmetry. So, point I is correct.  \n● The Constitution’s Fifth and Sixth Schedules carve out a separate zone of governance for certain areas \noccupied by indigenous peoples. The sixth schedule delegates a certain degree of power to local, self-\ngoverning institutions such as Autonomous District Councils. Such territorially tailored autonomy is a \nkey form of asymmetric federalism. So, point II is correct.  \n● The Indian Constitution includes special provisions for certain states, recognizing the unique needs \nand circumstances of these regions. It reflects the asymmetric nature of Indian federalism. So, point III \nis correct.  \n● UTs with legislatures face different restrictions. For example, between Puducherry and Delhi, there is \na notable difference. Puducherry has legislative powers on any matter mentioned in the State List or the \nConcurrent List, insofar as it applies to the Union Territory. Delhi, which has the same field, has three \nfurther exceptions: police, land and public order are outside its purview. So, point IV is not correct.  \nTherefore, option (c) is the correct answer."
